# Signing / secrets
#
# These are PATTERNS, not exact names, on purpose: the rules used to name
# `telecampro-upload-passwords.txt.gpg` literally, so every ordinary variant escaped them — a
# `.2026-07-07.bak` copy of that very file showed up as committable during the 2026-08-02 keystore
# recovery. A secret that leaks because it was renamed is still a leaked secret.
*.jks
*.keystore
*.gpg
*.asc
*.p12
*.pem
*.pfx
keystore.properties*
!keystore.properties.example
# Extension patterns alone are not enough: a `*.gpg.2026-07-07.bak` copy ends in `.bak`, so it
# matched none of them. Match on the NAME too, and on the archive suffixes people reach for when
# they set a secret aside "for a moment".
*password*
*secret*
*credential*
telecampro-upload-*
*.bak
*.orig

# OS
.DS_Store

# Logs
*.log

# OMC
.omc/

# Local agent/context scaffolding (not part of the shipped project)
.context/
.claude/

# Local reference sample photos from the stock camera (EXIF reference only — never commit)
IMG2*.heic
IMG2*.jpg
device-tests/reports/
.tool-state/
__pycache__/
*.jks.*
*.keystore.*

# Internal working docs and agent artifacts — kept locally, not part of the open-source tree.
# docs/assets/ and docs/licenses/ stay tracked: the Pages logo, Play art, and the Inter OFL
# (which must ship with the bundled font) are public on purpose.
/docs/*.md
/docs/plans/
/docs/reviews/
/docs/superpowers/
# Device-harness source is versioned; only generated evidence and caches stay local.
/device-tests/reports/
/device-tests/**/__pycache__/
/device-tests/**/.pytest_cache/

# Signed release artifacts kept locally so a `gradle clean` cannot destroy the exact bytes
# that were device-verified. Binaries never enter git history — Play distributes them.
releases/
